const r=13,e="Study and Management of Agricultural Residues",a="Manya Maurya, Safa Zai, Sushmita Saroj",t=[164,171],i=["Department of Zoology","T.D.P.G. College Affiliated by VBSPU Jaunpur U.P. (222002) India.","Corresponding author:","Email: manyamaurya2020@gmail.com"],s=[{t:"h",x:"Abstract:"},{t:"p",x:"Agricultural residues are generated after harvesting crops such as wheat straw, rice straw, sugarcane bagasse, and paddy stubble etc. The objective of this study was to analyze the types of agricultural residues, the problems associated with them, and to explore sustainable management practices. This study focuses on the management and reuse of these agricultural residues. These agricultural residues cause serious problems for farmers and the environment such as air pollution, loss of soil fertility, destruction of beneficial soil micro-organisms and reduce of soil nutrients. This study discusses the management of agricultural residues in two ways: In-situ management and Ex-situ management. In-situ management includes mulching, incorporation into soil and zero tillage etc. And Ex-situ management includes composting, vermicomposting, bioenergy production, as animal fodder and mushroom cultivation. The study also discusses advanced information regarding the management of agricultural residues, such as their role as circular bio-economy resources, carbon sequestration, use in bio-refineries, and microbial decomposition technology etc. We also discussed modern technique of management of agriculture residues for Bio char production. Finally, the study highlights the benefits of proper residue management. The study concludes that effective management of agricultural residues is essential for agricultural productivity and environmental protection. Converting residues into bioenergy, compost, and soil fertilizers ensures long-term soil fertility and environmental sustainability. Key words: Residues; In-situ & Ex-situ management; Bio-char Production; Sustainability."},{t:"h",x:"INTRODUCTION"},{t:"p",x:"Currently, India generates approximately 250 million tons of solid waste annually from industrial, mining, municipal, agricultural and other process. These of, 350 million tons of waste is from agricultural sources. Recycling is essential to prevent all pollution. This is proposed through the following points. Agriculture is one of the most important sectors supporting human life, rural livelihoods, and national economies, especially in agrarian countries like India. Along with the production of food grains, fruits, vegetables, and cash crops, a large quantity of by-products is also generated during cultivation, harvesting, threshing, processing, and storage. These by-products, commonly known as agricultural residues, include crop straw, husk, stalks, leaves, shells, cobs, bagasse, and other leftover plant materials (Long et al., 1998). Crop residues management is a major problem, and its proper handling has a positive impact on the environment. Crop residues are very important for the protection of soil and water. They are also used for feeding animals, preparing organic manure, and as fuel. However, despite these uses, there are many difficulties in managing crop residues (Mittal et al., 2009). Due to the rapid development of modern agriculture, especially in developing countries like India, the production of crop residues has increased"},{t:"h",x:"CHAPTER 13"},{t:"h",x:"STUDY AND MANAGEMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESIDUES"},{t:"p",x:"significantly. Farmers often find it difficult to manage these residues properly. Research clearly shows that when crop residues are left on the soil surface, they help in maintaining soil fertility and improving soil structure. They also reduce soil erosion and conserve moisture. In modern agricultural practices, machines are used for most farming activities, which makes work easier and faster. However, this has also led to an increase in the amount of crop residues. As a result, proper management of these residues has become an important necessity (CARB, 1997)."},{t:"p",x:"Due to changes in weather patterns, farmers often burn crop residues, which leads to pollution. Burning crop residues release several harmful gases into the atmosphere. These gases are dangerous for living organisms and also destroy beneficial microorganisms are unable to survive the high temperature of fire and get killed (Hays et al., 2005). Therefore, it is very important to manage agricultural residues in a scientific and proper way so that we can support sustainable development. Crop residues can be effectively used by converting them into organic manure and com0post through biological methods. In addition, residues can also be used to produce biogas and as a source of biofuel. Apart from this, many useful products can be made from agricultural residues, such as paper, cardboard, packaging materials, and other raw materials. Improper management of crop residues not only causes environmental pollution but also reduces soil fertility and overall productivity (Gadde et al., 2009)."},{t:"p",x:"Objective: After studying the agricultural wastes and their management system, its main objective is to make the agricultural waste materials mainly for human use and to keep the environmental ecosystem safely. Overall this is main objectives. The present study on agricultural residues and their management has been undertaken with the aim of understanding the nature, significance, and practical utilization of crop-based waste materials generated during agricultural activities. The main objective of this study is to understand the problems related to crop residue management and to find effective solutions for them (Pathak et al., 2010). Through this study, we aim to analyze the causes of improper management of agricultural residues and their impact on the environment. Another objective is to make farmers aware of better and scientific methods for managing crop residues so that environmental pollution can be reduced. This study also focuses on promoting sustainable agricultural practices by encouraging the proper use of residues. In addition, the study aims to explore different techniques such as composting, biogas production, and the use of residues can be converted into useful products and resources (Singh and Sidhu, 2014)."},{t:"p",x:"Overall, the objective is to reduce environmental damage, improve soil fertility, and ensure better agricultural productivity through proper residues management. Proper management of agricultural residues is essential for environmental protection and sustainable development. If residues are managed properly, they can be used in many beneficial ways instead of being wasted or burned. Crop residues can be used as organic fertilizers, which improve soil fertility and enhance crop productivity. They can also be used for preparing compost and vermicompost, which are environmentally friendly and beneficial for soil health (Gupta et al., 2004). In addition, agricultural residues can be used to produce bioenergy such as biogas, which serves as an alternative source of energy. These residues can also be utilized in industries to make useful products like paper, cardboard, packaging materials and other raw materials."},{t:"p",x:"The use of modern technologies and scientific methods can further improve the efficiency of residue management. It is important to create awareness among farmers about these methods so that they can adopt sustainable practices. The aim is create awareness about the importance of proper management of agricultural residues. This study presents and useful methods for proper management of agricultural residues and encourages awareness and encourages economically beneficial and environmentally responsible conservation (Setter et al., 2020)."},{t:"p",x:"Need / Significance of the study - The study of agricultural residues has become increasingly important in the present context of sustainable farming, environmental protection, and efficient resource utilization. In agricultural countries like India, farming activities generate a large amount of leftover biomass in the form of straw, stalks, husk, leaves, shells, and other crop residues. Traditionally, many of these materials were either reused at the household or farm level, but with changes in cropping patterns, mechanization, and increasing pressure on land use, a large portion of these residues is now left unmanaged or disposed of in harmful ways. This situation has created the need for systematic study and scientific management of agricultural residues. Definition of agricultural waste- According to the USEPA, Agricultural waste is the by- product of raising animals and the production and harvest of crops or trees. “Animal waste is a large component of agricultural waste which includes waste from livestock, dairy and other animal- related agriculture and farming practices”. Or When fertilizers, pesticides, fungicides and other chemicals are used excessively to increase agricultural production, the environment becomes polluted. Thus, agricultural waste is generated due to the use of fertilizers, pesticides and fungicides. The waste that pollutes the environment is called residues (USEPA, 2017)."},{t:"p",x:"Nature of agricultural waste- Its component structure According to this, agricultural waste can be of both organic and non- organic types."},{t:"p",x:"Organic Waste- Animal waste, Crop residues, Fruit and Vegetables residues etc."},{t:"p",x:"Inorganic waste- Paper, Plastic, Rubber, Glass, Metal etc."},{t:"p",x:"Composition of agricultural waste – Agricultural waste includes waste from crops and animals, which is called agricultural waste Agricultural waste is a waste material produced entirely from agricultural waste (Mirsky et al., 2012)."},{t:"p",x:"Agricultural residues- Straw, Husk, Sugarcane baggases, paddy stubble, weeds, grass stubble, crop residues, maize, cob, peel, maize tree, cow dung and biogas residues etc. (Yat et al., 2008). Animal remains- Waste from domesticated animals this includes dung, Urine etc. Apart from this, the non-edible parts of the carcass of animals like feathers, bones horns etc. are also included in the slaughter house waste material (Kumar et al., 2015)."},{t:"p",x:"Production of agriculture waste- On the basis of the source of origin of Agricultural residues, residues has been classified as follows-Field waste material Production in the field"},{t:"l",x:["Straw, husk, Sugarcane bagasse Crop residues","Weeds & Grass Uncultivated or wild vegetation","Animal wastes Produced by various animal activates","Dead body Post mortem arrangements for animals","Animal dung, rotten fodder Produced by various animal activates"]},{t:"p",x:"Agricultural industrial waste Production during various processing and production activities"},{t:"p",x:"Source of Agricultural waste material and their importance: The main sources of waste are usually worm farming and animal husbandry, from which we primarily obtain waste materials. You can manage and use it like-"},{t:"l",x:["• Stubble/ chaff","• Biogas Production","• Vermi-compost production","• Dairy and poultry","• Aquaculture and pisiculture etc."]},{t:"p",x:"Stubble (The residue of wheat crop or paddy crop) - After harvesting, farmers take the crop which is of no use to them and burn it to clear the field for next sowing, which pollutes the environment. Now days people use machines for paddy harvesting, which cuts only the upper part, leaving more of the lower part, and farmers, in a hurry for the next crop, burn it, preparing both crops on time. However, parali management can be done specifically according to the environment stubble can be cut by a small machine attached to a tractor and spread in the field using a rapet. This will provide natural fertilizer to the upcoming crop, and beneficial microorganisms will grow in the parali residues, increasing the soil’s fertility."},{t:"p",x:"• Alternatively, stubble can be uprooted using machines and collected in a pit (2-3feet deep) , where it can be decomposed using anaerobic bacteria by adding a mixture of Jaggery, Sugar urea, Cow dung and water producing organic manure."},{t:"p",x:"• Stubble can be used for producing electricity, a part from being used as fodder and for making cardboard."},{t:"p",x:"• It also aids in the production of various vegetables like turmeric, onion, garlic and chili."},{t:"p",x:"• By managing stubble, we can also utilize it for mushroom production."},{t:"h",x:"Biogas Production"},{t:"p",x:"Biogas is a renewable energy source like solar and wind energy. Producing biogas as a fuel from organic waste decomposition will undoubtedly benefit the environment. These benefits includes reducing methane emissions from livestock and enterprises. And generating green energy. For biogas production, toilets can be connected to reactors and co-digested with animal dung . However, biogas production from human waste is relatively low, making animal dung and green waste essential to meet current demands."},{t:"p",x:"Vermicompost Production (Worm manure)"},{t:"p",x:"To Produce vermi-Compost, first collect various organic wastes like crop residues, grass weeds agricultural industry waste and the biodegradable part of urban and rural waste. Select a suitable earthworm species and release them into a pit at the vermi-culture site. The earthworms feed on organic matter like plant or animal urine, dairy and cow dung."},{t:"p",x:"Importance- Vermicompost is rich in essential nutrients, improves soil structures aeration and prevents soil erosion. It’s free from pathogens, toxic elements, and weed seeds. Vermicompost also reduce insect and disease incidence."},{t:"p",x:"Dairy and Poultry- Dairy farming is the branch of agriculture that involves raising dairy animals to meet human energy needs like food items with milk products. Poultry farming is the farming of poultry, which produces human food items like eggs, chickens and chicken related products. The dairy industry produces large quantities of waste products, such as the water left over useless and are from cottage cheese and curd. These are largely released into the environment. However, these can be managed and utilized. Similarly, poultry feathers, their excrement, and their leftover feed can also be managed and put to use. We discard these wastes into the environment, considering them useless, thus polluting the environment. Organic fertilizers can be produced , primarily be managing dairy and poultry by-products. They contain some nutrients such as calcium, magnesium, copper, iron, zinc and sodium."},{t:"h",x:"Aquaculture & pisiculture"},{t:"p",x:"Aquaculture and piscivory produce aquatic organisms that provide significant benefits to humans, but they also produce waste products that primarily pollute water. We can reduce and manage the waste products produced by these cultures making them more reusable. It will be beneficial. By feeding them at the right time and in the right manner, waste can be reduced. The water containing their excreta is used in agriculture area (e.g. paddy crop) can be used for irrigation."},{t:"h",x:"Agricultural waste management"},{t:"p",x:"To manage agricultural waste, mainly six basic tasks have to be done which are as follows-Production – Production is the quantity and nature of agricultural waste generated by an agricultural enterprise waste management is required. When production from any sources is substantial. The waste management system may need to accommodate variable. Variations in the rate of production. The focus should be on minimizing unnecessary waste rather that the management process with simply determining (Wang et al., 2007)."},{t:"p",x:"Collection- This initial stages involves collecting and collecting waste. A waste management system plan should identify the method of collection the location of collection points, the scheduling of collection, the waste management requirements the necessary equipment and the cost and effectiveness of collection. Storage- A storage facility is a tool that allows managers to determine the timing and scheduling of system operations, including favorable weather and field conditions for further processing. Treatment- Treatment may be physical, biological and chemical. Treatment is an important process for reducing the polluting potential of waste. IT also includes activities that are sometimes, considered hazardous. Such as the separation of waste materials the characteristics of which must be analyzed before disposal. Also, it is necessary to evaluate the type of treatment facility, estimated size, location cost selection, management of the treatment process etc."},{t:"p",x:"Transfer- It refers to the movement and transportation of waste throughout the system. This includes the movement of waste from collection points to storage, treatment facilities and disposal sites. A waste management system includes the waste & location, transportation methods, necessary equipment and management analysis."},{t:"h",x:"Uses"},{t:"p",x:"Recyclable after use at all points of use recycling involves recycling waste products and returning non-recyclable waste products to the environment. Agricultural wastes can be used as a source of energy, animal feed, mulch, organic matter, or plant nutrients. When treated poorly, they may be marketable and valuable."},{t:"h",x:"Uses of agricultural wastes"},{t:"h",x:"Serial No. Name of waste Use"},{t:"p",x:"1."},{t:"p",x:"Rice, cotton and wheat husks of plants To be used in mixing with mud in making mud houses."},{t:"l",x:["2.","Sugarcane bagasse Making roofs of mud houses, in","making paper.","3.","Sawdust As fuel in homes","4.","Leaves of plants fruits & vegetables of","the peelings","The organic matter is then crushed","with sand or soil for 45-50 minutes","to make compost.","5."]},{t:"p",x:"Chicken wings with victory over animals In making bushes, in making fans."},{t:"l",x:["6.","Horns of animals When and how to make toys &","decorative material.","7.","Cereal husks Straw, for feeding animals.","8.","Plastic, Polythene (sacks etc.). In carpet, rope and road","construction."]},{t:"p",x:"Ecological importance of waste – Now days, old and old pesticides are being used in agriculture. These are eliminating not only the pests than harm crops but also the friendly insects. Another reason is climate change, its profound impact can be seen especially in tropical regions. To avoid all these effects, we should use organic fertilizers made from agricultural waste in our crops, which will protect our environment and maintain the ecosystem (Mirsky et al., 2012)."},{t:"h",x:"Conclusion"},{t:"p",x:"Agricultural residues are an unavoidable outcome of farming activities, but they should not be regarded merely as waste materials. The present study shows that these residues, if managed properly, can become valuable resources for agriculture, industry, and environmental protection. Crop residues such as straw, stalks, husk, leaves, shells, and bagasse are produced in large quantities from different agricultural operations, and their effective utilization is essential for sustainable rural development. Therefore, the management of agricultural residues should be considered an important part of modern agricultural planning. Farmers need proper awareness, affordable technologies, and institutional support to adopt sustainable residue management practices. Government agencies, researchers, and educational institutions also have an important role in promoting scientific methods and reducing the harmful effects of residue mismanagement. In conclusion, agricultural residues should be seen not as a burden, but as a useful biological resource with multiple benefits. Their proper study and management can help build a cleaner environment, healthier soils, and a more sustainable agricultural future. Effective residue management is thus essential for balancing agricultural productivity with ecological responsibility. After studying the activity waste management, we have learnt the following things."},{t:"p",x:"• Waste materials can be reused by making them useful according to their condition."},{t:"p",x:"• Waste and useless materials can be converted into new ones and made ready for use, and this process is called recycling."},{t:"p",x:"• Proper management is necessary for disposal and recycling of waste materials."},{t:"p",x:"• Waste management is the responsibility of both the government and local bodies as well as organizations and individuals."},{t:"p",x:"• Community participation and public co-operation are essential for proper management of waste materials."},{t:"h",x:"References:"},{t:"p",x:"CARB (1997). ‘Alternative uses of rice-straw in California’, Final Report, California Air Resources Board (CARB), California Eagle."},{t:"p",x:"Gadde, B., Menke, C., Wassman, R. (2009). Rice straw as a renewable energy source in India, Thailand and the Philippines: Overall potential and limitations for energy contribution and greenhouse gas migration. Biomass and Bioenergy, 33:11."},{t:"p",x:"Gupta, P.K., Sahai, S., Singh, N., Dixit, C.K., Singh, D.P., Sharma, C., Tiwari, M.K., Gupta, R.K., Garg, S.C. (2004). Residue burning in rice-wheat cropping system: Causes and implications. Current Science, 87(12):1713-1717."},{t:"p",x:"Hays, M.D., Fine, P.M., Geron, C.D., Kleeman, M.J., Gullett, B.K. (2005). Open burning of agricultural biomass; physical and chemical properties of particle-phase emissions. Atmospheric environment, 39:6747-6764."},{t:"p",x:"Kumar, P., Kumar, S., Joshi, L. (2015). Socioeconomic and environmental implications of agricultural residue burning: A case study of Punjab, India. Springer Nature, 144."},{t:"p",x:"Long, W., Tate, R.B., Neuman, M., Manfreda, J., Becker, A. B., Anthonisen, N.R. (1998). Respiratory symptoms in a susceptible population due to burning of agricultural residue. Chest, 113(2): 351- 357."},{t:"p",x:"Mirsky, S.B., Ryan, M.R., Curran, W.S., Teasdale, J.R., Maul, J., Spargo, J.T., Moyer, J., Grantham, A.M., Weber, D., Way, T.R., Camargo, G.G. (2012). Conservation tillage issues: Cover crop-based organic rotational no-till grain production in the mid-Atlantic region, USA. Renewable Agriculture and Food Systems, 27(1):31-40."},{t:"p",x:"Mittal, S.K., Susheel, K., Singh, N., Agarwal, R., Awasthi, A. and Gupta, P.K. (2009). Ambient air quality during wheat and rice crop stubble burning episodes in Patiala. Atmospheric environment, 43:238- 244."},{t:"p",x:"Pathak, H., Bhatia, A., Jain, N., Aggarwal, P.K. (2010). Greenhouse gas emission and mitigation in Indian agriculture-A review. ING Bulletins on Regional Assessment of Reactive Nitrogen, Bulletin, 19:1-34."},{t:"p",x:"Setter, C., Silva, F.T.M., Assis, M.R., Ataíde, C. H., Trugilho, P.F., Oliveira, T.J.P. (2020). Slow pyrolysis of coffee husk briquettes: Characterization of the solid and liquid fractions. Fuel, 261:116420 Singh, Y., Sidhu, H.S. (2014). Management of cereal crop residues for sustainable rice-wheat production system in the Indo-Gangetic plains of India. Proceedings of the Indian National Science Academy, 80(1): 95-114."},{t:"p",x:"USEPA, 2017. Our Mission and What We Do. EPA January 21, 2017. Archived from the original on March 28, 2017. Retrieved Mach 28, 2017"},{t:"p",x:"Wang, M., Wu, M., Huo, H. (2007). Life-cycle energy and greenhouse gas emission impacts of different corn ethanol plant types. Environmental Research Letters, 2(2):024001."},{t:"p",x:"Yat, S.C., Berger, A., Shonnard, D.R. (2008). Kinetic characterization for dilute sulfuric acid hydrolysis of timber varieties and switchgrass. Bioresource technology, 99(9):3855-3863."}],n={n:13,title:e,authors:a,pages:t,affiliations:i,blocks:s};export{i as affiliations,a as authors,s as blocks,n as default,r as n,t as pages,e as title};
